            introduction: 'Building wealth is not about luck, inheritance, or winning the lottery. It is a predictable process based on three variables: your savings rate, your investment returns, and time. The formula is simple: earn → save a significant percentage → invest that savings wisely → wait. The most important variable is actually your savings rate — the percentage of income you save and invest, not your total income. Someone earning ₹8 lakh/year who saves 30% (₹2.4 lakh/year) will build more wealth than someone earning ₹25 lakh/year who saves 5% (₹1.25 lakh/year). The second critical concept is "lifestyle inflation" — the tendency to increase spending as income rises. When you get a promotion, if you immediately upgrade your car, rent a bigger flat, and eat at fancier restaurants, your savings rate never improves. The wealthy build wealth not by earning more (though that helps), but by maintaining a high savings rate regardless of income level. The final piece is avoiding major financial mistakes: carrying high-interest credit card debt, buying a car that costs more than 50% of your annual income (a ₹6 lakh car when you earn ₹10 lakh is okay, but a ₹15 lakh car is not), buying too much house, and falling for "get rich quick" schemes like gold doubling schemes or cryptocurrency hype without research.',
