            introduction: 'Something is "overpriced" when its price exceeds its perceived value. But "value" is subjective — it depends on context, alternatives, and your personal situation. A ₹50 bottle of packaged water at a railway station feels overpriced when the same bottle costs ₹20 at a local shop. But if you are dehydrated on a hot platform and the next stall is 500 metres away, that ₹50 suddenly feels reasonable. Markup is the difference between what something costs to produce and what it sells for. Restaurants typically mark up food 300%, clothing 200-400%, and jewellery can be marked up 500% or more. Brand premiums are what you pay for the logo — a plain kurta costs ₹500 to make, but a designer label sells a similar one for ₹5,000. Location matters too: a cup of chai at a roadside stall costs ₹10, at a normal café ₹30, and at a five-star hotel ₹250. The "overpriced" question comes down to three things: Can you find a better alternative? Does the price reflect genuine quality or just branding? And most importantly — does this purchase align with your financial goals? A ₹5,000 dinner might be overpriced for a college student but perfectly reasonable for a family celebrating an anniversary.',
